  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 216 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold front will bring light rain showers to portions \nof northern New York and the higher terrain of Vermont this morning \nand early this afternoon. Model soundings continue to show a \nplethora of dry air in the low levels which will likely inhibit \nmuch, if not all, rainfall from making it into the deeper valleys. \nStill, any location that does receive rain today will see well under \na tenth of an inch and will only be for a brief period of time. High \npressure will quickly build across the region Tuesday with strong \ncold air advection leading to noticeably cooler temperatures on \nTuesday. For example, we will see temperatures warm into the mid 50s \nto mid 60s today but see temperatures struggle to warm into the mid \nto upper 40s on Tuesday. Several locations in Vermont have the \npotential to see record lows Wednesday morning as temperatures fall \ninto the mid teens to lower 20s. This cool down will be short-lived \nas warm advection will begin on Wednesday and will allow for \ntemperatures to moderate back into the lower 60s by Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: With the exception of a few chances for showers Thursday \nnight, mostly dry and seasonably warm weather is expected for \nlate this week into the weekend. Another weak cold front looks \nto move through the region late Thursday, with the potential for\nsome light rain showers, particularly across northern portions \nof the forecast area. Heading into the weekend, drier weather \nlooks to prevail, especially for Saturday. Temperatures later \nthis week into the weekend look to be seasonably warm, with \ndaytime high temperatures climbing into the mid 50s and lower \n60s and overnight lows falling into the 30s and 40s.",
